---
/**
 * NAMED CASE STUDIES — Full approved copy, v1.
 *
 * The page's premise is that clients are named. AFIMAC's published cases are
 * anonymized, so four of the five rows carry a bracket where the client name
 * goes and cannot ship until permission is on file. That is the page's whole
 * open item, so it leads rather than sitting in a footnote.
 */
import Prototype from "../../layouts/Prototype.astro";
import Block from "../../components/Block.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/base";
import { B } from "../../data/nav";
import { photo } from "../../lib/wireframe";
import { CASE_STUDIES, CASE_SHEET_LEAD, caseCard } from "../../data/case-studies";
import type { Block as BlockType } from "../../lib/types";

/**
 * The six travel-labor cases come from the approved sheet, through
 * `src/data/case-studies.ts`, so this page cannot drift from the overview
 * carousel or the industry pages the way the five paraphrases here used to.
 * The Qualcomm testimonial is not in that sheet and is kept as its own row.
 */
/**
 * The six travel-labor cards come straight from the approved v1 sheet through
 * `caseCard`, so this page, the overview carousel and the industry pages all
 * render the same design from the same words. The Qualcomm testimonial is not
 * in that sheet and keeps the older logo row, which is the right shape for it:
 * it is a named client with a quote, not a figure and a deployment.
 */
const CASE_CARDS = CASE_STUDIES.map(caseCard).join("");

const READ_HREF = u("/resources");

const QUALCOMM = [
  '<div class="casecard" style="margin-top:18px">',
  `<div style="max-width:160px">${photo("Client logo · SVG or PNG @2×, 160px wide max · greyscale on the row, colour on hover")}</div>`,
  '<div><div class="blocknum">Qualcomm · Technology · Protective Services</div><h3>Executive protection, sustained</h3>',
  '<p>“Our experience with AFIMAC has been nothing short of outstanding. The team has consistently demonstrated professionalism, reliability, and a high level of expertise in all aspects of security management.” Ronnie Lopez, Qualcomm Executive Protection Manager.</p>',
  `<a class="readmore" href="${READ_HREF}">Read the case »</a></div>`,
  "</div>",
].join("");

const blocks: BlockType[] = [
  {
    cls: "dark",
    n: "BLOCK 01 · HERO",
    h: `
  <p class="eyebrow">Resources</p>
  <h1>Case Studies</h1>
  <p class="lead">The situation, what AFIMAC deployed, and what happened. Real clients, named with their permission.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:28px">${photo("Band crop from a 2560×1440 JPG source, displayed at roughly 2560×800 · a working floor mid-shift, crew at stations · muted tone under the navy scrim")}</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The eyebrow is “Resources”, not “Critical Situation Travel Labor · Resources”</b> like its two siblings. Deliberate if the page is moving up a level; an inconsistency if it isn’t. Same decision as the one above.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The sub-nav calls this <i>Named</i> Case Studies; the H1 is just <i>Case Studies</i>.</b> A shorter H1 than a nav label is normal, but here the dropped word is the one carrying the page’s whole premise — and the premise is the thing that is blocked. Worth aligning them.</div>`,
    spec: `<b>Elementor:</b> Container (bg image + navy overlay) · Heading ×2 · Text Editor. Compact utility band, matching the other resources pages.`,
  },

  {
    n: "BLOCK 02 · THE CASE STUDIES",
    h: `
  <p class="lead">${CASE_SHEET_LEAD}</p>
  <div class="note">Optional filter chips above the list: <b>All · Travel Labor · Protective Services</b>. Worth having once the travel-labor rows outnumber the protective-services one, which they now do six to one.</div>
  ${CASE_CARDS}
  <div style="margin-top:34px"><div class="blocknum">PROTECTIVE SERVICES</div>${QUALCOMM}</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The cards are the v1 sheet's design</b> — the figure in coral on the left, the two category tags, the client, the story — rather than the logo-and-summary row this page used to run. <b>Seven cases, up from five:</b> the sheet carries six travel-labor cases and this page had five, four of them shorter paraphrases. The two it never had are the building materials manufacturer and the second food manufacturer.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The Qualcomm row keeps the old shape.</b> It is not in the sheet, and it is a named client with a quote rather than a figure and a deployment, so a card built around a number would have nothing to put in it.</div>
  <div class="note stop"><b>Two approved sources word these cases differently, and this page follows the sheet.</b> The paraphrases it used to run came from the pillar, and the note on them read: “these are the sentences legal has already seen.” The designed sheet is also AFIMAC-approved and is fuller, so it wins on completeness — but <b>if the pillar's wording is the one that cleared legal, this swap has to be checked before build</b>, not after. The old text is in the history of this file.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The logo column is gone from the six.</b> The sheet's design has no logo slot, which quietly removes the thing that was blocking them — but only from the layout. <b>The permission problem is unchanged</b>: these clients are anonymised, and the brackets came off because the design stopped asking for a name, not because anyone granted one.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>“Read the case” has no destination yet.</b> Either build per-case detail pages or expand the row in place. The deck leaves it open; a five-row list with five links to nothing is the one outcome to avoid.</div>`,
    spec: `<b>Elementor:</b> Container (single-column list) · EK Icon Box ×5 · optional EK Filter Gallery for the chips. Logos need written permission on file — <b>each one, individually</b>.`,
  },

  {
    cls: "sky",
    n: "BLOCK 03 · CTA STRIP",
    h: `
  <div style="text-align:center">
   <h2 style="margin-bottom:18px">Facing something like this? Tell us about it.</h2>
   <div class="btns" style="justify-content:center"><a class="btn" href="${u("/get-in-touch")}">Talk to AFIMAC</a></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note"><b>No form on this page, by design.</b> The strip routes to the contact page instead — the only resources page that does. Keep it that way; a case-study reader is further along than a library browser.</div>`,
    spec: `<b>Elementor:</b> Container (full-width strip) · Heading · Button. Global block candidate — <a href="${u("/resources/sales-sheets")}">Sales Sheets</a> runs the same strip with different wording.`,
  },

  // A note about the page, after the page.
  {
    n: "SCOPE · WHAT BLOCKS THIS PAGE",
    h: `
  <div class="note stop" style="margin-top:0"><b>Six of the seven rows cannot ship as written</b>, and the new sheet does not change that. This page exists to name clients; every case AFIMAC publishes is anonymised — Fortune 500 manufacturer, agricultural equipment manufacturer, food manufacturer, switchgear manufacturer, building materials manufacturer. Each needs <b>written permission to be named and logo\u2019d</b> before it runs. <b>Qualcomm is the only client currently named in public</b>, and that row is a testimonial rather than a case study.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>A page that ships with six bracketed placeholders is worse than a page with one Qualcomm row.</b> The realistic launch is the Qualcomm row plus whichever of the six clears legal — so treat the permission chase as the critical path, not the build. <b>More cases makes that chase longer, not shorter.</b></div>
  <div class="note stop"><b>And this page may not belong under CSTL at all.</b> Row 05 is protective services. If more protective-services cases are approved, the deck says the page may belong one level up — which would take it out of the <a href="${u("/launch-phases")}">CSTL rollout</a> entirely.</div>`,
    spec: `Permissions are a legal task with a lead time. Start it now; the build is a day and the approvals are not.`,
  },
];
---

<Prototype
  title="Case Studies"
  crumb="Solutions › CSTL › Resources › Case Studies"
  url={`${B}resources/case-studies/`}
  status="build"
  active="/resources/case-studies"
  meta={{
    t: "Client Case Studies & Measurable Results | AFIMAC Global",
    d: "Named case studies from AFIMAC clients: the situation they faced, what AFIMAC deployed, and the measurable result, across travel labor and protective services.",
    k: "AFIMAC case studies · travel labor case study · workforce deployment results · executive protection case study · manufacturing labor shortage case study",
  }}
>
  {blocks.map((b) => <Block {...b} />)}
</Prototype>
